System libraries in the Linux wheels
------------------------------------

On Linux, OCCT's font manager uses fontconfig, and `auditwheel repair` bundles
it, together with the system libraries it needs, from the manylinux_2_28 build
image (AlmaLinux 8). They are **used unmodified**, as the distribution builds
them:

    fontconfig 2.13.1       licenses/fontconfig.txt
    FreeType 2.9.1          licenses/FreeType-FTL.txt
    libpng 1.6.34           licenses/libpng-1.6.34.txt
    bzip2 (libbz2) 1.0.6    licenses/bzip2.txt
    libuuid (util-linux)    licenses/libuuid.txt

FreeType's own licence file adds that its contributed BDF and PCF drivers come
with a licence similar to that of the X Window System (src/bdf/README and
src/pcf/README in its source).

They are the AlmaLinux 8 packages of the build image, whose `dnf install` takes
the current el8 release of each; these notices do not record the exact release a
particular wheel carries. Their licences are permissive and do not require
distributing their source; it is available as the AlmaLinux 8 source RPM of each
package (freetype, fontconfig, libpng, bzip2, util-linux), at
https://vault.almalinux.org/8.10/BaseOS/Source/Packages/ for 8.10.


Microsoft Visual C++ runtime in the Windows wheel
-------------------------------------------------

The Windows wheel bundles msvcp140.dll, the Microsoft Visual C++ runtime the
extension modules need, unmodified, from the Visual C++ runtime folder of the
Visual Studio 2026 that builds the wheel. It is Microsoft's and not open source:
it is redistributed as Visual C++ Runtime "Distributable Code" under the
Microsoft Software License Terms for Visual Studio, not under nanocct's licence.

The following file records the runtime's provenance, identifies the applicable
Microsoft terms and links to them; it does not replace or grant rights beyond
